From db46602f96e771d08697637c4758aacb2d1b77eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 02:34:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/18] logging: write log lines on a logger thread

An enabled log line took the logger's one mutex and wrote to the log
file (one unbuffered write) under it, on the thread that logged:
validation, message handling, network. A slow disk stalled every
logging thread. With 8 threads logging to a file, the median call took
13 us and the 99th percentile 460 us (max 17 ms).

Each thread now has its own single-producer ring of fixed-size records
(1024 x 512 bytes, slots not zeroed so only used pages are touched). The
caller copies the time, level, category, thread name, source location
and message into a slot and publishes it: no lock, no system call and
no allocation in the common case. A message longer than a slot (~450
bytes) is moved, not copied, into a heap string referenced by the
record. A logger thread renders the records (text or JSON), oldest first
across threads and in order for each thread, writes them through a
fully buffered file and flushes after each pass: every 2 ms while busy,
20 ms when idle, or at once when a ring gets half full.

When a ring is full, debug and info lines are dropped and counted
(never blocking the caller); the logger writes a line saying how many,
and lotus_log_dropped_lines_total exports the count. Warnings and errors
are never dropped: the caller waits for room, writing the queue itself
if the logger thread is busy. Errors are written through (the caller
writes the queue up to its line), and the queue is also written at
shutdown, at exit and before an assertion aborts, so crash logs are
complete. Lines logged before StartLogging are buffered as before.

Other hot-path costs go too: Enabled() and the category/level check no
longer take the mutex (per-category levels are an array of atomics), so
a disabled debug line costs one relaxed load. Threads get their ring when
they start (util::ThreadRename); rings of exited threads are reused.

Options apply when lines are rendered, and print callbacks run on the
logger thread: tests that read the log or count lines through a callback
now call LogInstance().Flush() first (DebugLogHelper does).

Caller cost (one thread, short line incl. its tfm::format): median 208
ns, p99 459 ns, against 1.9 us / 6.7 us before. 16 threads: median 125
ns, p99 292 ns, against 6.3 us / 1 ms. Writing costs the logger thread
73 ns (short) to 178 ns (300 bytes) per text line.
